# xlayer

An educational compatibility layer, in the spirit of Wine and Proton, for x64 PE programs that
import Xbox GDK system libraries such as `kernelx.dll`. The plan: load such a program with our
own PE loader and hand it our own implementations of those libraries, written on top of the
ordinary Win32 API, so it runs on desktop Windows.

This is a learning and portfolio project. It is built in small, tested steps, from a PE inspector
up to a loader, and then to growing API coverage.

## Ground rules

These rules are part of the project, not suggestions:

1. **No DRM circumvention.** No code that decrypts content, bypasses copy protection, or handles
   XVC packages.
2. **No Microsoft code or binaries.** No leaked SDKs and no Microsoft Xbox DLLs. Every function
   prototype is written by us from public documentation (such as the PE/COFF specification) and
   from observing our own test programs.
3. **Own test programs only.** Everything is tested on programs we compile ourselves (`tests/`),
   and later on homebrew.
4. **No commercial titles.** The project never names or targets specific commercial games.

## Status

| Phase | What | Status |
|------:|------|--------|
| 0 | `xinspect`: PE inspector and API coverage tracker (C#) | done |
| 1 | Test executables and a PE loader with logging stubs (C++20) | done |
| 2 | `xkernel`: real implementations (memory, threads, synchronisation), 61 kernelx functions | done |
| 3 | Virtual file system and process identity (3a); basic runtime: initialisation, local user (3b) | done |
| 4 | Input (own GameInput subset: XInput or replay) and audio (XAudio2 from Windows) | done |
| 5 | Graphics: D3D12 pass-through, with a d3d12.dll overlay to choose the GPU (`--gpu`) | done (GPU code translation: long-term) |

## Requirements

- Windows 10/11 x64
- [.NET 10 SDK](https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download) for xinspect
- Visual Studio 2026 with *Desktop development with C++* (MSVC and the Windows SDK), and
  CMake 4.2 or newer (needed for the Visual Studio 2026 generator) for the loader

## xlayer (the loader)

```powershell
cmake --preset default
cmake --build --preset debug
ctest --preset debug
build\bin\Debug\xlayer.exe --verbose build\bin\Debug\ret42.exe
```

`xlayer` maps the program into its own process, resolves its imports, and calls its entry
point. Imports of Xbox DLLs come from **xkernel**, xlayer's own implementation: 84 kernelx.dll
functions (memory, threads, synchronisation, time, files, process), 8 xgameruntime.dll
functions (runtime initialisation, XAsync, one local offline user) and gameinput.dll (the
current gamepad state, from a real controller through XInput or from a replay file). Imports of
other DLLs come from Windows; audio, for example, is Windows' own XAudio2, and graphics is
Windows' own D3D12. For d3d12.dll, xkernel is an *overlay*: it replaces only
`D3D12CreateDevice`, so `--gpu` can choose the GPU, and every other function is Windows'. Functions neither provides get logging stubs that return 0. Every step and
every stub call goes to `xlayer.log`; the stub summary at the end lists what to implement next.

The program sees itself, not xlayer: `GetModuleHandle(NULL)` is its own image,
`GetModuleFileName` says `G:\<program>.exe`, and `GetCommandLine` holds the arguments given
after the program. It also sees a sandboxed virtual file system:

| Drive | Is | Access |
|-------|----|--------|
| `G:\` | the program's folder (and its starting current directory) | read-only |
| `T:\` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\xlayer\<program>\` | read/write |

Anything outside the mounted drives does not exist for the program. Device, UNC and `\\?\`
paths and DOS device names such as `NUL` are refused.

```powershell
build\bin\Debug\xlayer.exe --mount T:=C:\temp\saves build\bin\Debug\files.exe alpha "two words"
```

| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `-v`, `--verbose` | Print every loader step to stderr. |
| `--log <file>` | Log file (default: `xlayer.log` in the current directory). |
| `--force-relocate` | Never use the preferred ImageBase, so base relocations must be applied. |
| `--mount X:=<dir>` | Mount a directory as drive X: (read/write, created if missing). Replaces a default drive with the same letter. |
| `--mount-ro X:=<dir>` | Mount an existing directory as drive X: (read-only). |
| `--user <name>` | Name of the local offline user that `XUserAddAsync` signs in (default `Player`). No network, accounts or licensing are involved. |
| `--input-replay <file>` | Take the gamepad from a replay file (format in [docs/architecture.md](docs/architecture.md), section 7.7) instead of a real controller. |
| `--native <dll>` | Take a DLL from Windows even though xkernel provides it, e.g. `--native gameinput.dll`. |
| `--gpu <choice>` | The GPU for D3D12: `default` (the program's choice), `warp` (Windows' software renderer, the same result on every PC) or `hardware` (high performance). |

A visible demo of D3D12 through xlayer (Esc closes it):

```powershell
build\bin\Debug\xlayer.exe build\bin\Debug\graphics_window.exe 10
```
| `--list-builtins` | List the functions xkernel provides (`<dll> <function> <win32\|own> <category>`). |

Options go before the program; everything after it is passed to the program. The exit code is
the program's own, or `125` (bad command line), or `126` (loading failed; the message names the
step). If the program crashes, the log names the exception and the crash address, and xlayer
exits with the exception code.

How the loader, the IAT and the stub trampolines work is described in
[docs/architecture.md](docs/architecture.md).

## xinspect

`xinspect` reads a PE file and shows what it asks of the system: headers, sections, imported
DLLs and functions (including delay-load imports), and how much of that xlayer already provides.

```powershell
dotnet build
dotnet test
dotnet run --project tools/xinspect -- C:\Windows\System32\notepad.exe
dotnet run --project tools/xinspect -- C:\Windows\System32\notepad.exe --json
```

| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `--json` | Print a machine-readable report (no absolute paths or timestamps, so reports can be diffed over time). |
| `--only-unknown` | Show only DLLs that are not standard Windows DLLs. |
| `--apis <dir>` | API database directory. By default xinspect uses the nearest `apis` directory above the current directory, or above its own location. |

For example, `dotnet run --project tools/xinspect -- build\bin\Debug\ret42.exe --only-unknown`
shows the `kernelx.dll` import of our test program.

Imports by ordinal are named from the export table of the DLL on this PC, so
`d3d12.dll!#101` shows as `#101 (D3D12CreateDevice)`. A DLL that is not in the Windows system
directory leaves the ordinal as it is.

Exit codes: `0` ok, `1` usage error, `2` unreadable or invalid image, `3` invalid API database.

Only x64 (PE32+) images are supported. Other formats and damaged files are reported with a clear
error message; the tool does not crash on them.

### Function status and coverage

Every imported function gets one of these statuses:

| Status | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `system` | Exported by a standard Windows DLL. The loader passes it through to Windows, so it is not counted in coverage. |
| `implemented` | xlayer has a real implementation. |
| `stub` | xlayer has a placeholder that logs the call and returns a dummy value. |
| `missing` | Not provided yet. Functions of DLLs that are neither system nor in the database are all `missing`. |

Coverage is `implemented / (implemented + stub + missing)`, per DLL and in total.

`apis/kernelx.json`, `apis/xgameruntime.json`, `apis/gameinput.json` and `apis/d3d12.json` list
exactly what xkernel provides; the CTest check `apis.match_xkernel` fails if they drift apart.
A file with `"overlay": true` describes a Windows DLL of which xlayer replaces only the listed
functions; xinspect counts its other functions as `system`.

### API database (`apis/*.json`)

One file per DLL that xlayer re-implements:

```json
{
  "dll": "kernelx.dll",
  "functions": {
    "SomeFunction": "implemented",
    "OtherFunction": "stub",
    "#12": "missing"
  }
}
```

DLL names are case-insensitive. Function names are case-sensitive. Ordinal imports use the key
`"#<ordinal>"`. Functions not listed count as `missing`.

## Repository layout

```
CLAUDE.md               working rules for the AI assistant
CMakeLists.txt          C++ build (loader and test programs); CMakePresets.json
docs/architecture.md    how PE parsing, the loader, the IAT and stubs work
tools/xinspect/         PE inspector (C#, .NET 10)
tools/xinspect.Tests/   xUnit v3 tests
apis/                   API database
loader/                 PE loader (xlayer_core library) and launcher (xlayer.exe), C++20
xkernel/                our implementation of kernelx.dll (static library), C++20
tests/                  our own test programs, kernelx.def, CTest checks
```

